 Cleaning out the mid-week notebook: 

 🤮 DPI despair: Venture capital returns still suck, despite the AI boom, per a new Carta analysis of nearly 3,000 funds. 

 (I'm sorry. Was that too crass? I mean: The venture capital asset class is underperforming traditional return models.) The vast majority of funds raised in the past decade have not yet returned their initial commitments, let alone generated excess returns. 

 Only the 90th percentile of 2017 and 2018 vintage funds is above 1.0x DPI, at 1.37x and 1.12x, respectively. 75th percentile funds for those vintages don't make the cut. Yes, the percentage of funds with at least some DPI is rising, but those are quite literally pennies on the dollar. Not surprisingly, there's a growing divergence between TVPI and IRR, with the former rising and the latter falling. 🛢️ Not so fast: Carlyle early this year agreed to buy most foreign assets of Russia's Lukoil, which had been hammered by U.S. sanctions , but now it may have conflicted competition from the U.S. government itself.

 Billionaire investor Todd Boehly has secured backing for a rival bid from the U.S. International Development Finance Corp. (led by Leon Black's son Ben) and UAE national security adviser Tahnoon bin Zayed al-Nahyan, per the FT . As the FT writes: "Boehly's proposal would put the U.S. government in the unusual position of simultaneously bidding for the assets while another part of the administration decides their fate." ❓ Just asking: Private equity has been furiously buying and rolling up RIAs, but could it be doing long-term damage to the industry?

 Wealth advisory is something of an apprenticeship business, where younger advisors work hard with the expectation of inheriting older advisor economics. But PE-backed consolidation can put a crimp in those plans, thus reducing younger advisor incentives... 💰 Fund scoop: Captain Ventures, a new firm led by Tyler Hogge (ex-Pelion Venture Partners), raised $33 million for its debut fund.

 The pitch to founders (or "captains") is that Salt Lake City-based Hogge worked at three unicorns before becoming a VC, and that he can help Silicon Valley startups source Utah talent. The pitch to LPs was that Captain will focus on getting into top startups, regardless of being able to lead (which it hopes becomes a virtuous cycle). 🎟️ Reminder: You can request an invite to Axios BFD, taking place in NYC on Nov. 17, by going here .

 
 
